course: dictionary
lesson: 14942
title: Rehouse: a new home
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To rehouse someone is to give them a new home. People are usually rehoused because their old home is unsafe, has been destroyed, or is being knocked down.
explain: Look at the clues in «After the flood, the council had to rehouse the families who lost their homes.» The words who lost their homes tell you the old homes are gone, so the families need new ones. The prefix re- means again, and house means home, so rehouse means to house again.
explain: Animals can be rehoused too. «The shelter worked hard to rehouse the dogs before winter.» Here it means finding each dog a new home with a family. Always check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «The shelter worked hard to find the dogs new homes» still makes sense, so you have it right.
fact: Rehouse is a verb: rehouse, rehouses, rehoused, rehousing.
fact: «Rehoused» means given a new home, often because the old one was unsafe or destroyed.
fact: Councils rehouse families after floods or fires, or when old flats are pulled down.
fact: Animal shelters rehouse pets by finding them new homes.
fact: The prefix re- means again, so rehouse means to house again.
exercise: «After the fire, the council had to rehouse the family.» What does rehouse mean here? | To burn their house down | To visit them each day | *To give the family a new home | To paint the house again
exercise: Animal shelters rehouse pets. What does rehouse mean for a pet? | *To find it a new home | To give it a bath | To take it for a walk | To feed it every day
exercise: «The old flats were pulled down, so the families were rehoused.» Find and copy the word that tells us the families got new homes. | families | pulled | *rehoused | flats
test: «The families were rehoused after the flood.» Why were they rehoused? | They won a prize | Their old homes were painted | *Their old homes were unsafe or destroyed | They wanted a holiday
test: The prefix re- in rehouse means again. What does rehouse mean, put together? | Back and road | Not and home | *Again and home, so to house again | Very and big
exam: «The dogs were rehoused before the winter.» What does rehoused mean? | Taken to the vet only | Made to walk further | Sent to sleep outside | *Given a new home
